{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"content_hash":"222ece408dd69c6ab71291cafb4211c7daf2f8f4fe807df4117c0fe8685de9ce","digest":{"component_or_area":"rebar reconciler jira sanitizers","key_entities":["JIRA_LABEL_MAX_CHARS","sanitize_label","InvalidLabelError","adapters/jira_family/sanitizers.py","tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_characterization.py","tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_dc_backend_characterization.py","tests/scripts/bridge/test_bridge_acli_field_coverage.py","sanitize_summary","comment_max_chars"],"problem_keywords":["label ceiling","jira dc","jira cloud","sanitization","silent failure","truncation","raise vs truncate"],"propositions":["Jira DC and Cloud were suspected to have different label character ceilings (DC 254 vs Cloud 255), but live measurement on both deployments shows they both accept 255 characters and reject 256","sanitize_label raises InvalidLabelError above the limit while sanitize_summary truncates with visible suffix, creating inconsistent failure modes that must be explicitly documented","the original capability map claiming DC rejects 255-char labels was incorrect and has been retracted","tightening the shared JIRA_LABEL_MAX_CHARS constant to 254 caused four characterization oracle tests to fail because it would make Cloud reject valid 255-char labels","a per-deployment configurable label ceiling was proposed but is not needed since no actual divergence between deployments exists"]},"digest_hash_version":1,"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8","schema":"ticket_digest_v1"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"TICKET_DIGEST","timestamp":1787891333646638001,"uuid":"132dbdb6-dffe-43fa-98ff-d8c70e1e4041"}